# Horo SDK compatibility and migration — edition 1

This guide ships with extension SDK **@HORO_EXTENSION_SDK_VERSION@**, engine
**@HORO_ENGINE_VERSION@**, source revision **@HORO_SOURCE_REVISION@**. Read it and
the sibling [machine-readable matrix](CompatibilityMatrix.json) directly from
an extracted SDK archive; no engine source checkout or network connection is
needed. The edition versions this document format, not the native ABI.

## Select an exact snapshot

The engine is in development. Several incompatible gameplay snapshots use the
same `0.1.0` engine/extension SDK version. A version string is not a release
attestation or a unique archive identity. Preserve the engine revision, SDK ZIP
SHA-256, platform, toolchain/configuration and generated descriptor revision.
`unknown` revision means provenance is unavailable, not verified compatibility.
An SDK built before this guide existed must be identified from its retained
archive/provenance; do not assign it the current snapshot's contracts.

| Contract in this archive | Version / admission |
|---|---|
| Engine and extension SDK | @HORO_ENGINE_VERSION@ / @HORO_EXTENSION_SDK_VERSION@; revision @HORO_SOURCE_REVISION@ |
| External native host ABI | `horo-extension` @HORO_EXTENSION_ABI_MAJOR_VERSION@.@HORO_EXTENSION_ABI_MIN_HOST_MINOR@ inclusive to @HORO_EXTENSION_ABI_MAX_HOST_MAJOR_EXCLUSIVE@.0 exclusive; declarations through @HORO_EXTENSION_ABI_MAJOR_VERSION@.@HORO_EXTENSION_ABI_PUBLISHED_MINOR@ |
| Extension descriptor | `extension.json`, schema 1 |
| Package manifest | `horo-package.toml`, schema 1; owns package identity/dependencies |
| Gameplay C++ boundary in this engine snapshot | Native boundary 9 / `gameplay-sdk9`; separate exact-fingerprint build-tree SDK |
| Author CI platform profiles | Linux x64, macOS arm64, macOS x64, Windows x64 |

The external extension range does not imply that every table/capability works on
every host in that range. Require the minor and callbacks actually used. Platform
profiles are supported build/test routes, not a claim that release archives exist
or that this snapshot has passed every platform. The extension SDK has no engine
library link dependency; native libraries still require matching OS/architecture
and their own private dependency/toolchain compatibility. No Android, mobile,
cross-architecture, or generic C++ binary compatibility is promised here.

## External C ABI 1.0 → 1.1

The major stays 1. Load/unload entry points and the original host-table prefix
remain supported. The optional inert `horo_extension_query` negotiates minimum
host minor, required functions and table size before load. Missing query means
legacy minor 0, never an inferred modern profile. Modules needing negotiation
should add the query and also check requirements in load for older hosts.

Source migration: include the packaged `Horo/Extensions/ExtensionAbi.h` through
`HoroEngine::ExtensionSdk`. Status/setting wire tags use the public `uint32_t`
typedefs rather than compiler-sized enum tags; replace explicitly spelled old
enum tags with those typedef names and rebuild. Nonstandard short-enum binaries
are outside the compatibility promise. Never transfer STL, exceptions, allocators
or C++ deletion across this boundary. A non-null importer context needs its
module destroy callback; failed registration leaves ownership with the module.
After accepted registration, host-owned leases keep the originating code alive.

An existing binary using the original compliant C prefix does not need a rebuild
merely because the host minor increased. A changed source/wire representation or
new required callback does. Rollback: retain the old exact SDK/header and binary,
remove new-minor requirements/features, then validate against the older host;
do not relabel a new-minor binary as legacy.

## External C ABI 1.1 → 1.2 → 1.3

Minor 2 adds platform-service provider registration. Minor 3 adds the provider
operation lifecycle; neither changes the major or earlier host prefixes.
Version-1 provider descriptors must retain the original prefix size:
`offsetof(HoroPlatformServicesProviderDescriptor, operations)`. Replacing it with
new-header `sizeof` would advertise an unimplemented tail.

To use operations, require host minor 3, use provider descriptor version 2 and its
full size, and provide a non-null, exact-version-1 `HoroPlatformProviderOperations`
table. Rebuild that provider. Old factory-only providers can still load but the
operation host reports `platform.lifecycle.unsupported_profile`; loading is not
operation availability. Providers map completion codes to
`HoroPlatformProviderResultCode`, not private SDK codes; unknown values become
Unknown. This semantic fix does not change the completion layout. Failure
payloads must not contain private error text, credentials or account identifiers.

The module retains its native code/candidate and must close ingress and drain
before retire/destroy. BUSY is retried on the owner lane; cancellation is not proof
of drain. Rollback to a 1.2 host needs the old factory-only binary and original
descriptor prefix, with operation-dependent features disabled. Rollback to a 1.1
host also removes platform-provider contributions. Do not silently fall back to a
different provider or fabricate a successful service start.

## Gameplay SDK8 → SDK9 (native boundary 8 → 9)

This is a **breaking C++ layout/vtable and fingerprint change**, not extension
host ABI 1.4. `GameRuntimeContext` gains an injected Physics context and the
behavior backend gains its accessor. Rebuild every native gameplay module and
generated descriptor bundle with the same engine, compiler, platform and
configuration. Old boundaries/fingerprints are rejected before factory entry.
The separate gameplay SDK remains a build-tree export, not a relocatable installed
SDK supplied by this archive. This guide needs no checkout to read; producing a
gameplay module still needs that separate matching SDK.

Replacement access: include `Horo/Gameplay/GameplayPhysicsContext.h` through
`HoroEngine::GameplayPhysicsIntegration` and opt into the `Physics` component of
`HoroEngineGameplay`. Acquire the injected holder with its exact module, Scene ID
and activation generation. It returns `PhysicsQueryEventCapability`, not a world
pointer/native solver. Missing/denied context stays explicitly unavailable;
existing loader call expressions do not implicitly activate Physics. Lua's private
`ctx.physics.acquire()` returns a capability or `nil, {domain, code}`. Editor Play
Physics composition remains unavailable in this snapshot; SDK9 does not promise
editor Physics activation. Native Start/Stop, behavior/Lua adapters, headless
composition and external module projects must agree on the new boundary.

Rollback: stop the host and restore the complete SDK8 engine, matching module
binaries and descriptor bundles, project backup and locks. Rebuilding SDK9 source
against SDK8 requires removing the Physics-context/accessor use and its component
link; never load an SDK9 module into SDK8 by changing a manifest/fingerprint.

## Retirement source API migration in this snapshot

Host integrations use `ExtensionManager::RetireExtension`, inspect its attributed
retirement report, and call `FinalizeRetirements` only after admitted work has drained
on the owner lane. Registries can attach the typed retirement owner to actual
callback/job/resource/UI/service publications. Manager move operations were
removed: own it at a stable address (for example, `std::unique_ptr`) instead of
move-assigning into a live manager. This is a host C++ source change; the external
C ABI entry-point version is unchanged. Rebuild host consumers using that API.

Retirement withdraws publications and rejects later retained-snapshot calls while
admitted work pins provider/dependency code. BUSY, off-lane or surviving-work
teardown can report `RestartRequired` and retain native code for the process
lifetime; this is deliberately not leak-free unload or `Complete`. The manager's
1024-entry bound is per manager, not globally bounded across failed recreation.
Disable/update/remove remain restart-default. Rollback requires a stopped host
and the previous coherent host/package set; never force-unload retained code.

## Offline upgrade and rollback checklist

1. Retain the previous engine artifact/revision, SDK ZIP/digest, package binaries,
   lock, configuration and project backup outside the candidate staging area.
2. Read the candidate archive's matrix and this version-specific guide. Compare
   major/minor/table requirements, schema, platform and gameplay fingerprint.
3. Update the author project's SDK lock with exact archive URLs and SHA-256 for
   each selected platform. An archive already retained locally can be inspected
   offline; the supplied CI bootstrap downloads pinned archives and is not an
   offline restore tool. No placeholder digest is a release attestation.
4. Rebuild only the affected contracts described above, then run the packaged
   manifest validator and trusted-native conformance harness before packaging.
   They do not grant trust or prove full package lifecycle activation.
5. Stop the old host, stage the coherent candidate and obtain explicit local
   trust approval for its exact identity. Restart and check real functionality.
6. On failure, stop again, restore the retained coherent set and project backup,
   re-evaluate trust and restart. Preserve failure diagnostics. This is a manual
   safe rollback procedure, not an invented transactional package-upgrade API.

Do not infer compatibility from “latest”, load order, successful compilation,
archive integrity alone, or a version string shared by development snapshots.

## External C ABI 1.3 → 1.4 editor activities

Minor 4 appends `registerEditorActivity`; the 1.3 host prefix ends at
`offsetof(HoroExtensionHostApi, registerEditorActivity)`. Old modules retain their
prefix sizes and negotiate independently. Activity modules require minor 4 and
`HORO_EXTENSION_REQUIRES_EDITOR_ACTIVITY`, include `EditorActivityAbi.h`, declare
exact `editor.activity_item` and `editor.panel` claims and require the host
`editor.activity` capability. Headless and uncomposed editor hosts reject the
request explicitly. The generic conformance tool has no editor publication host;
its rejection of this required profile is expected and does not prove GUI support.

Session version 1 advertises a finite node mask for standard text, label, action,
stack and group form nodes. Other standard controls need a later explicitly
negotiated transport. A package never supplies a draw callback or renderer handle.
Snapshots strictly increase revision and are copied synchronously on the owner
lane. Semantic actions run through the application job system with cancellation
and module-code leases; their borrowed result sink is valid only during that call.
Discard the session before module unload. Package icon resources use the bounded
static SVG profile documented in the engine migration guide.

The shared activity header keeps its C11 typedef spellings and exposes the same
struct names directly to C++ consumers; its action callback uses an equivalent
C++ type alias. These declaration-only differences do not alter transport layout,
numeric constants, callback signatures, or version negotiation. No package data
migration is required. Both the strict C11 consumer and C++ public-header consumer
check the append-only host prefix and callback contracts; the relocated installed
SDK consumer remains the packaging compatibility check.
